WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Spacing is the quietest part of a
layout and the easiest to get wrong.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.420
Most messy interfaces are not
badly coloured or badly typeset.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.400
They have twenty slightly
different gaps that nobody chose.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.340
In this lesson you will group with
proximity, pick an 8 point scale,

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.720
and let the parent own the gaps.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.620
People assume that things close
together belong together.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:29.600
The space between groups must be clearly
larger than the space within a group.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:35.840
A form label that sits 16 pixels above its
input and 16 pixels below the previous

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.220
input is ambiguous. Which
field does it belong to?

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.300
A reliable rule: the gap between groups should
be at least twice the gap inside a group.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.540
An 8 point scale gives you
a short menu: 4, 8, 12,

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:57.840
16, 24, 32, 48, 64. The 4 is for

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.880
tight details, like the gap
between an icon and its label.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:05.780
Everything else steps up in
a way that is easy to see.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.980
The value of a scale is not the number 8.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.380
It is that decisions become choices
from a list rather than guesses,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.400
and that two people working on the same
screen end up with the same answer.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.840
This settings row collected
margins over time.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.520
Thirteen, eighteen, five, twenty-two.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.660
None of those numbers were a
decision you could defend.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.200
They are leftovers.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.560
The row uses the scale, and
the parent owns the spacing.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.860
Inside the row, a 4 pixel gap keeps
the title and description together.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.160
The settings list uses a
24 pixel gap between rows,

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:49.920
which is more than twice the gap inside a
row, so each row reads as its own group.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.280
Padding is 16 and 24, both on the scale.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.620
Using gap on the parent, instead
of margins on the children,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.920
removes a whole class of bugs.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:05.620
No collapsing margins. No stray
space after the last item.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.980
No need to remember which
child carries the margin.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.060
Separate spacing into two kinds.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:17.000
Inside a component: padding and
the gap between its own parts.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:22.440
The component owns this. Between
components: the layout around it.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:28.640
The parent owns this. Components that set
their own outer margins are hard to reuse,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.180
because every new context
needs a different margin.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.900
When you hand over a design
or review a pull request,

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:41.180
annotate gaps with their
scale values: 8, 16, 24.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.860
If a number does not appear on the scale,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.720
it is either a bug or a sign
the scale needs a new step.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.840
Both are worth a conversation.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.640
Space between groups should be clearly
larger than space within them.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.900
Choose values from a short scale
rather than inventing new ones.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.880
Let parents own the gaps
between children, using gap.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.240
Next we stop counting gaps
and start counting edges:

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.460
a simple column grid, and alignment that
still holds when the words change length.
